  "grammarExplanation": "These verbs work back-to-front compared with English: the THING is the grammatical subject and the PERSON is an indirect object pronoun.\n\n• Me gusta el café = Coffee pleases me (= I like coffee)\n• Me gustan las aceitunas = The olives please me (plural thing → gustan)\n\nIndirect object pronouns:\n• me = to me • te = to you • le = to him/her • nos = to us • os = to you all • les = to them\n\nThe verb agrees with the THING: gusta/gustan, encanta/encantan, interesa/interesan, falta/faltan.\n\nThe family:\n• encantar = to love (stronger than gustar)\n• apetecer = to fancy / feel like\n• doler = to hurt (me duele la cabeza)\n• molestar = to bother\n• interesar = to interest\n• importar = to matter (no me importa = I don't mind)\n• faltar = to be missing / lacking\n• caer bien = to like (a person): Me cae bien tu hermana.",
